WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.713
A card fee calculation runs about six hundred thousand times a second.

2
00:00:07.713 --> 00:00:09.289
Each tick is one call.

3
00:00:09.289 --> 00:00:10.507
Taller is slower.

4
00:00:10.733 --> 00:00:14.096
Half the calls take under 0.7 microseconds.

5
00:00:14.096 --> 00:00:15.973
The average looks great.

6
00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:19.617
But one in ten thousand takes over 13 milliseconds.

7
00:00:19.617 --> 00:00:22.229
At checkout, that's a timeout upstream.

8
00:00:22.433 --> 00:00:24.573
What do the slow ones have in common?

9
00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:28.088
Each lands on a garbage collection pause.

10
00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:31.347
Every call leaves objects on the heap.

11
00:00:31.347 --> 00:00:33.760
When it fills, the collector pauses.

12
00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:34.900
So what fills it?

13
00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:36.652
Ignore the max.

14
00:00:36.652 --> 00:00:39.667
It stayed near half a second, fixed or not.

15
00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:42.847
Here is the loop from symptom to proof.

16
00:00:43.867 --> 00:00:49.292
JFR's allocation view names JDK methods, like Formatter.parse.

17
00:00:49.292 --> 00:00:51.304
You can't change those.

18
00:00:52.633 --> 00:00:56.651
So walk each sample's stack down to the first line of your own code.

19
00:00:57.367 --> 00:00:59.016
Now it's a to-do list.

20
00:00:59.016 --> 00:01:03.140
Over half of all allocation is a log line nobody reads.

21
00:01:03.367 --> 00:01:05.827
A flame graph of the same code agrees.

22
00:01:06.833 --> 00:01:09.573
Round one fixes the three obvious costs.

23
00:01:09.573 --> 00:01:12.382
Build the pattern and the constants once.

24
00:01:12.382 --> 00:01:15.259
Format the log line only when DEBUG is on.

25
00:01:15.467 --> 00:01:20.572
The tail drops from over 13 milliseconds to under 36 microseconds.

26
00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:22.437
Now profile again.

27
00:01:22.437 --> 00:01:23.437
What leads?

28
00:01:24.867 --> 00:01:26.724
A Matcher, made on every call.

29
00:01:26.724 --> 00:01:30.128
It was there from the start, hidden under bigger costs.

30
00:01:30.333 --> 00:01:32.939
Three letters don't need a regex.

31
00:01:32.939 --> 00:01:35.150
Round two uses a plain loop.

32
00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:36.967
Collections drop to 37.

33
00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:38.193
Profile again.

34
00:01:38.193 --> 00:01:40.604
What's left is parsing the amount.

35
00:01:40.604 --> 00:01:42.660
That's the real job, so stop.

36
00:01:43.467 --> 00:01:50.129
JMH proves it: 2,032 bytes per call, then 200, then 72.

37
00:01:50.633 --> 00:01:52.100
A test keeps it fixed.

38
00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:56.300
After warm-up, it fails if the bytes per call go over a budget.

39
00:01:56.533 --> 00:01:57.721
Leave a margin.

40
00:01:57.721 --> 00:02:00.728
The numbers move between JDK versions.

41
00:02:01.633 --> 00:02:04.157
Here's that test in Java and Kotlin.

42
00:02:06.467 --> 00:02:07.095
The rule.

43
00:02:07.095 --> 00:02:13.307
Start from the tail, find your line, fix one thing, profile again, and stop at real work.

44
00:02:13.533 --> 00:02:14.529
Check yourself.

45
00:02:14.529 --> 00:02:17.647
You hoist the Pattern but forget the log guard.

46
00:02:17.647 --> 00:02:19.505
What leads the next profile?

47
00:02:22.233 --> 00:02:23.760
The String.format line.

48
00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:26.349
It was about a third of all allocation.

49
00:02:26.349 --> 00:02:28.141
Now it's the biggest share.
